WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.720
On the 7th of October 1849, a heavily&nbsp;
intoxicated man was found lying in the gutter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.120
outside a pub in Baltimore. As is traditional with&nbsp;
intoxicated men in gutters, this one cut a fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.278
unimpressive figure. He was dressed in someone&nbsp;
else's clothes, his hair had been carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:16.480
styled to look exactly like toilet brush,&nbsp;
and he was muttering to himself incoherently.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.880
In other words, he could have been any&nbsp;
drunk in any gutter almost anywhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.040
the world. But this man wasn't just&nbsp;
anyone. He was Edgar Allan Poe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.000
one of the greatest writers who ever lived.&nbsp;
And exactly how he ended up in that gutter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.200
in 1849 remains one of the biggest&nbsp;
mysteries in the history of literature.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.280
Now, I know what you're thinking - isn't it&nbsp;
pretty bloody obvious how an extremely drunk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.720
man ended up in a gutter outside a pub. But, bear&nbsp;
with me. Because this plot is about to thicken.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.680
Poe was taken to the nearby Washington Medical&nbsp;
College for treatment, and it was there that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.360
something odd happened - or should I say,&nbsp;
*didn't* happen. Considering the state he was in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.200
everyone assumed Poe was raging drunk. But even&nbsp;
the drunkest of men will sober up sooner or later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:08.400
whereas Poe remained in the same delirious,&nbsp;
incoherent state for four days straight...

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.040
...and then he died.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.720
By all accounts it was a strange way&nbsp;
to go, especially since he was only 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.640
at the time. But things were about to&nbsp;
get stranger still. Because it turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.200
out that Poe had been missing for almost&nbsp;
a week before he was found in that gutter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:29.600
and to this day we have no idea where he went&nbsp;
or what he did in the meantime - he seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.680
have just... disappeared off the face of the earth&nbsp;
only to turn up half mad and die a few days later.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.840
It's hard to miss the irony that Poe's mysterious&nbsp;
death reads like something straight out of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:49.120
of his own stories, and there are some genuinely&nbsp;
wild theories about exactly what happened to him.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.520
But before we get onto Poe's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.280
we should probably talk about his&nbsp;
life. Because what a life it was.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.560
Most people think of Edgar Allan Poe&nbsp;
predominantly as a horror writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.880
but the truth is he was so much more than that.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.440
He single-handedly invented detective fiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.680
was one of the founders of sci-fi alongside&nbsp;
authors like Mary Shelley and Jules Verne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.840
and he was one of the first people to&nbsp;
explore the art of the short story.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.440
It’s also been largely forgotten that&nbsp;
before he took storytelling seriously Poe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.760
had an extremely promising career&nbsp;
in the military, and he might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.880
gone right to the top if he hadn't got&nbsp;
himself court-martialled... on purpose.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.800
He was also something of a polymath,&nbsp;
and despite never having any formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.720
scientific training he used the power&nbsp;
of his frankly frightening imagination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.920
to somehow predict the origin of&nbsp;
the universe and the existence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.000
black holes several decades before the&nbsp;
actual scientists came up with them.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.840
And then there’s what is probably my favourite&nbsp;
story about Edgar Allan Poe - one that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:25.040
fittingly enough, comes from beyond&nbsp;
the grave. I don’t want to spoil it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:31.400
but let’s just say it involves an accidental&nbsp;
prediction so utterly spectacular some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.200
believe Poe might just have been a time&nbsp;
traveller. Stick around till the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.960
the video and I'll fill you in on the&nbsp;
details. Trust me - it'll be worth it.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.440
For now, without further ado, this&nbsp;
is the remarkable, incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:49.680
near-unbelievable life story… of Edgar Allan Poe.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.600
Edgar *Poe* was born on the&nbsp;
19th of January 1849 in Boston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.080
Massachusetts. Early life for little&nbsp;
Edgar was... well, it wasn't great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.520
His father left the family home and never&nbsp;
came back when Edgar was just a year old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.680
and a year after that his mother Eliza died of&nbsp;
what is today assumed to have been tuberculosis.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.840
Just in case that wasn't a bad enough start&nbsp;
to life for Edgar and his two siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.480
they were then split up and sent&nbsp;
to live with 3 different families.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.160
Presumably thoroughly traumatised by&nbsp;
this point, Edgar ended up in Richmond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.480
Virginia with Scottish Merchant&nbsp;
John Allan and his wife Frances.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.720
He was never formally adopted, but&nbsp;
the Allans did have Edgar baptised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.960
and that's where he acquired the missing&nbsp;
third of that catchy name of his.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.720
The Allans were one of the wealthiest families in&nbsp;
all of Richmond, and Edgar was given an education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.280
befitting his new station, including a 5&nbsp;
year stint in the UK where he studied at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.600
some of the finest boarding schools around. In&nbsp;
1826 he attended the University of Virginia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.200
founded 7 years earlier by some&nbsp;
bloke called Thomas Jefferson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.320
where he studied ancient and modern languages.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.640
Poe had always been an excellent student, but like&nbsp;
many young men attending university for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:06.440
time, rather than concentrate on his studies he&nbsp;
decided to go absolutely bloody mental instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:12.320
Within a year he was forced to drop out after&nbsp;
amassing substantial drinking and gambling debts.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.360
That was pretty bad, but things were about to get&nbsp;
even worse. Because, like many filthy rich men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:24.240
John Allan absolutely detested the idea of&nbsp;
wasting money. In order to make sure his foster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.320
son couldn’t fritter away any more of it losing&nbsp;
at cards, Allan simply turned off the money tap.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:36.160
Which is why, at the age of 18, a flat broke Edgar&nbsp;
Allan Poe decided to join the army. Taking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.920
stereotype of writers being frail, bookish-types&nbsp;
and bayonetting it into a billion bloody bits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.360
Poe took to the army like a duck to&nbsp;
hoisin sauce. He made short work of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.080
the lowest rungs on the military ladder, and was&nbsp;
eventually selected from among 500 of his peers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:56.800
for promotion to sergeant major, the highest&nbsp;
rank possible for a noncommissioned officer.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.440
In 1829 he left the army to enrol as a&nbsp;
cadet at the United States Military Academy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.880
better known as West Point. But he lasted&nbsp;
less than a year after realising his true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:11.560
calling was not with war, but words.&nbsp;
Rather than just, you know, quit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:16.280
he decided to go out with a bit of a bang by&nbsp;
deliberately getting himself court-martialled.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.560
Exactly how he did it is&nbsp;
the subject of some debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.840
but according to legend he was dismissed after&nbsp;
turning up butt naked to a parade ground drill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.040
Sadly that story’s probably apocryphal -&nbsp;
most historians agree he simply ignored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:32.800
his duties until those in charge were left&nbsp;
with no option but to give Poe the heave-ho.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.360
With his military career behind him&nbsp;
he made the bold decision to dedicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.560
himself to writing full time. That might&nbsp;
not sound like such a big deal these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.920
but back then it was practically unheard of.&nbsp;
Poe is widely accepted as having been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:51.480
first American writer to make a living&nbsp;
solely from writing - in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:56.600
he quit a promising career in the military&nbsp;
to do a job that didn't even exist yet.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.640
Perhaps unsurprisingly, it… didn't go all that&nbsp;
well. It wasn't that he found it difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:07.840
find buyers for his stories - he didn’t. The&nbsp;
trouble was they paid like shit. Copyright law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:13.440
was still in its infancy, meaning rather than&nbsp;
pay local writers a fair price for their work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.680
American magazines could simply print&nbsp;
stories by British authors for free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.400
There was precious little point paying&nbsp;
Poe a pretty penny when you could lift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.440
literature straight from the limeys.&nbsp;
Trying saying that ten times fast.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:30.320
With stories and poems not yet putting&nbsp;
food on the table - let alone paying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.080
rent - in 1835 Poe got himself a job&nbsp;
as a staff writer and critic at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:41.040
*Southern Literary Messenger* in Richmond.&nbsp;
The chance to earn a steady - albeit fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.600
pitiful - wage was a major breakthrough in&nbsp;
Poe’s career... or at least it would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.400
been if he hadn't got himself fired inside of&nbsp;
the first month for being drunk on the job.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.000
Not that he let unemployment get him down - he put&nbsp;
his new-found spare time to good use by finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:06.440
love. And when I say ‘love’ I mean he... married&nbsp;
his cousin. His... 13-year-old cousin. Yeeeeeah.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:11.160
The happy cousins--I mean couple -&nbsp;
were wed by a minister named Amasa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.840
Converse. That random fact has no&nbsp;
bearing whatsoever on this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:20.160
but I had to include it because history&nbsp;
should never have forgotten that neckbeard.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.080
A month after getting the boot from&nbsp;
the *Messenger* Poe was rehired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.880
and this time things went much more&nbsp;
smoothly. Not only was he able to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.360
many of his own stories and poems&nbsp;
published, he also started to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.360
a name for himself as a literary critic.&nbsp;
An.. *absolutely savage* literary critic.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.200
Over the next few years he bounced&nbsp;
around several different publications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:45.760
writing stories and poems and just generally&nbsp;
eviscerating the work of his colleagues for fun.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:51.360
In 1841 he casually invented an entirely new&nbsp;
genre of fiction when he published a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.720
story called *The Murders in the Rue Morgue.*&nbsp;
It was both the first modern detective story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.920
and the first locked room mystery, and it&nbsp;
featured a character called C. Auguste Dupin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.280
an eccentric but brilliant amateur&nbsp;
detective who uses his almost supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:10.240
analytical abilities to solve crimes.&nbsp;
If that's sounding just a tad familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.920
it's probably because a certain rather&nbsp;
famous fictional detective was very clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:20.200
inspired by Poe's creation - without Dupin,&nbsp;
there would have been no Sherlock Holmes.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.840
By the way, it's thought that Poe came up&nbsp;
with the idea for Dupin while doing a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.600
of detective work himself in figuring out the&nbsp;
trick behind the chess-playing automaton known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:32.840
as the mechanical turk. I covered the Turk&nbsp;
back in 2020 and it's one of my favourite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:36.320
videos on the channel - I highly recommend&nbsp;
you check it out if you haven't already.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:42.200
In 1843 Poe entered a story called The Gold-Bug&nbsp;
into a national competition. It won the grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:47.000
prize, pocketing him a cool hundred dollars&nbsp;
- probably the most he was ever paid for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:53.200
single piece of work. But its impact was far more&nbsp;
than just financial. In fact, you could say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:59.040
Gold-Bug was indirectly responsible for saving&nbsp;
tens, perhaps hundreds of thousands of lives.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.440
A key element of the story’s plot involved&nbsp;
a real cryptogram that, once solved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:09.280
revealed the location of some good old&nbsp;
fashioned buried treasure. Cryptography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.320
wasn’t well known in those days, and in the&nbsp;
decades after its publication the Gold-Bug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:18.440
would introduce tens of thousands of young&nbsp;
readers to the concept for the first time.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:24.240
Among them were Leo Marks and William Friedman,&nbsp;
both of whom read Poe’s stories as children and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:31.480
found the idea of cryptography so fascinating that&nbsp;
in later life they went on to work in the field.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.320
Leo Marks became chief cryptographer&nbsp;
for the Special Operations Executive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.280
a secret organisation credited by General&nbsp;
Eisenhower with shortening the Second World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.880
War by at least 3 months, and William&nbsp;
Friedman, along with his wife Elizebeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:51.040
went on to become one of the most influential&nbsp;
codebreakers in history. As the chief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.080
cryptanalyst at the Signal Intelligence&nbsp;
Service during the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.080 --> 00:12:01.360
his teams broke multiple Japanese ciphers,&nbsp;
saving countless lives in the process.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:07.400
In 1845, Poe's now legendary poem the Raven&nbsp;
was published in the New York Evening Mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:13.280
and was an instant hit. It's often reported&nbsp;
that Poe died penniless and in obscurity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.880
but only half of that is actually true -&nbsp;
like most of his work he didn't make much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.520
money from the Raven, selling it for just $9.&nbsp;
But it immediately struck a chord with readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:28.960
all over America and beyond, transforming&nbsp;
Poe into a household name almost overnight.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:34.200
To this day, the Raven remains one of the&nbsp;
most famous and best-loved poems of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.720
as well as one of the most quoted -&nbsp;
and parodied - pieces of literature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:42.160
in history. Even now, references to the&nbsp;
iconic poem pop up all over the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:46.600
from the Simpsons, to the NHL, and&nbsp;
from League of Legends to Star Trek.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:51.640
Three years after the Raven Poe published an&nbsp;
essay called Eureka. It was a major departure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:56.160
from his typical style, covering, of all&nbsp;
things, his ideas about the makeup of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.520
universe itself. You might think that’s&nbsp;
a fairly unusual area of interest for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:05.880
a writer with zero scientific training, and&nbsp;
plenty of people at the time agreed. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:12.320
Eureka was so downright odd that some viewed it&nbsp;
as compelling evidence Poe was losing his marbles.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:15.440
Little did they know that,&nbsp;
many decades after Poe’s death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.240
Eureka would prove to be one of the&nbsp;
most unexpectedly insightful essays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:25.560
ever written. That might sound like a pretty&nbsp;
big statement, but, well.. see for yourself.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:30.240
In the mid 19th century when Eureka was written,&nbsp;
the overwhelming scientific consensus was that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:36.760
universe was infinite, unchanging, and eternal.&nbsp;
But Eureka describes a dynamic, ever-shifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:44.280
universe born from a single primordial particle&nbsp;
that expanded outwards to create all matter. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:51.520
other words, Edgar Allen Poe predicted the Big&nbsp;
Bang almost 80 years before scientists did.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:56.240
Hidden within the complex labyrinth that is&nbsp;
Eureka are also allusions to the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.400
between energy and matter, the role&nbsp;
of the speed of light in the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:07.240
the existence of black holes, and the concept of a&nbsp;
cyclical universe that’s born and reborn over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:12.600
over again - it would be decades until any of&nbsp;
these ideas appeared in scientific literature.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:17.160
Eureka is so remarkable that even today&nbsp;
scholars aren't really sure what to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:22.840
with it. Alongside all the brilliant bits&nbsp;
Poe also got plenty wrong. But what he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:29.160
right is uncanny. Normal people just shouldn't&nbsp;
be able to randomly guess the deepest darkest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:35.080
secrets of the universe just by having a&nbsp;
bit of a think while sitting on the toilet.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:40.880
Eureka turned out to be one of Poe's last&nbsp;
major works. On the 27th of September 1849&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:45.080
at the age of just 40, he got on a train&nbsp;
in Richmond to go to New York where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:51.520
was living at the time. For the following&nbsp;
week nobody has any idea whatsoever what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:58.680
happened to him until he turned up in that&nbsp;
Baltimore gutter days before his death.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:03.640
One theory is that he may have been subjected to&nbsp;
something called 'cooping,' a kind of political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:08.240
fraud in which victims were kidnapped and forced&nbsp;
to vote for certain candidates in elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.720
often multiple times while&nbsp;
wearing a variety of disguises.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:16.480
That might sound, well, mental. But cooping was&nbsp;
actually quite common in Baltimore at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.560
and it would at least explain some&nbsp;
of the stranger details of the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:25.000
like why Poe was in Baltimore in the first place&nbsp;
and why he was wearing someone else's clothes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:29.880
Many victims of cooping were beaten or plied&nbsp;
with alcohol or drugs, a combination of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:36.280
might also explain Poe’s apparent delirium when&nbsp;
he was found, and perhaps even his cause of death.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:39.360
Cooping is certainly one of the most&nbsp;
popular theories about Poe’s death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:42.600
and disappearance, but it isn’t the only one.

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.200
Poe was buried in an unmarked&nbsp;
grave in a Baltimore graveyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:51.280
but as the years went by and his popularity&nbsp;
as a writer continued to grow, it was decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:57.840
that he needed a more fitting resting place. In&nbsp;
1875 a fancy monument was erected nearby, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:04.160
Poe’s remains were exhumed so that they could be&nbsp;
reburied at the new site. When his body was moved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:11.440
the local gravedigger claimed he could clearly&nbsp;
hear Poe’s brain rattling around inside his skull.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:14.360
At the time nobody thought anything much about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.920
but these days we know the gravedigger’s&nbsp;
story makes no sense - there’s simply no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:23.560
way a human brain could have survived 26&nbsp;
years underground without decomposing. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:29.000
if it wasn’t Poe’s grey matter bumping&nbsp;
around inside his head… what was it?

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:33.800
Well, it’s possible that little lump may&nbsp;
have been the calcified remains of a brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:39.120
tumour. If that’s true it might explain&nbsp;
Poe’s odd behaviour and delirium in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.680
days before his demise, and it would&nbsp;
also give us a clear cause of death.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.520
There have been literally dozens&nbsp;
of other theories over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:52.680
including everything from mercury poisoning to&nbsp;
rabies or even murder. But the truth is we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:58.600
likely never know for sure what exactly&nbsp;
killed Edgar Allan Poe. And perhaps it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:05.480
fitting that the death of the great mystery&nbsp;
writer will itself forever remain a mystery.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:11.680
And speaking of mysteries, it’s time I told&nbsp;
you about the greatest coincidence in history.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:19.080
In his all-too-short life, Poe wrote precisely&nbsp;
one novel - The Narrative of Arthur Gordon Pym of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:25.240
Nantucket. The book tells the story of a stowaway&nbsp;
on board a whaling ship called the Grampus.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:30.520
At some point the Grampus is badly damaged in&nbsp;
a severe storm that only 4 of the crew survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:35.480
and over the course of the following few weeks&nbsp;
adrift at sea, they slowly begin to succumb to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:40.040
starvation. The crew manage to bring a few&nbsp;
provisions up from the flooded depths of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:45.920
ship - including a small tortoise - but it&nbsp;
isn’t enough. And with no land in sight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:50.600
nothing else to eat, they eventually turn to&nbsp;
cannibalism. They draw straws, and the loser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:56.720
is the youngest survivor, a boy called Richard&nbsp;
Parker, who is killed and eaten by the others.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:02.880
The novel was published in 1838 and&nbsp;
it was largely a flop, but almost 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:07.640
years later something incredibly weird happened.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:12.720
In 1884, a yacht called the Mignonette sank&nbsp;
somewhere in the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:18.760
Just like in Poe’s novel, four men survived&nbsp;
the sinking, and (just like the novel) soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:23.520
enough they were starving. As luck would have&nbsp;
it, they managed to catch a turtle, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:28.320
fresh meat didn’t sustain them for long (OK so&nbsp;
it isn’t quite a tortoise but close enough).

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:32.640
Eventually the survivors grew so&nbsp;
desperate they decided one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:36.960
should be sacrificed for the good of&nbsp;
the others. And - just like in Poe’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:43.760
novel - it was the youngest member of the&nbsp;
crew who was chosen, and ultimately eaten.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:46.080
Pretty remarkable right?

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.440
But I know what you're thinking -&nbsp;
it’s a pretty weird coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:55.040
but… biggest in history? There must have been&nbsp;
dozens of cases of cannibalism aboard ships in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.040 --> 00:19:00.320
years since Poe died - it’s hardly a miracle that&nbsp;
one of them involved the same number of crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:05.040
a tortoise-slash-turtle, and the&nbsp;
youngest member being sacrificed.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:09.680
But… there's one piece of information&nbsp;
I haven't told you yet. Something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:15.440
transforms this from a quirky coincidence&nbsp;
into something more than a little bit spooky.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:19.280
I didn’t tell you the name of the&nbsp;
crew member who was eaten - just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:24.760
like in Poe’s story, his name was Richard Parker.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:28.240
What. The actual. F*ck.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:32.160
By the way, if that name is ringing any&nbsp;
bells it might be because the tiger in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:36.600
Life of Pi is also called Richard Parker&nbsp;
- author Yann Martel chose it as a kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:40.840
of easter egg to pay homage to the greatest&nbsp;
coincidence in literary history. (And, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:45.880
happen to have read the book or seen the film,&nbsp;
you’ll know exactly how fitting the name is.)

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:52.520
Edgar Allen Poe struggled for most of his life to&nbsp;
realise his dream of becoming an author. It’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:58.040
enormous shame that he never got to find out quite&nbsp;
how spectacularly that dream would eventually come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:03.920
true. Today he is universally regarded as&nbsp;
one of the greatest authors who ever lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:08.320
and his influence on modern fiction has&nbsp;
been so great that without him many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:13.080
the stories we know and love would never&nbsp;
even have existed. What a tragedy it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:19.560
that he died so young, to fill our&nbsp;
world with his stories… nevermore.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:22.840
Thanks for watching.

